kind of, almost daily.

00:21:23.470 --> 00:21:26.650
Where do you rank out
of 7.7 billion people?

00:21:26.650 --> 00:21:29.563
The answer, for a lot of
you, is outrageously high.

00:21:32.240 --> 00:21:34.030
If you're a white dude in here

00:21:34.030 --> 00:21:36.573
and your family has any
kind of financial security,

00:21:36.573 --> 00:21:38.483
you're in outer space.

00:21:40.770 --> 00:21:42.093
Outer fuckin' space.

00:21:43.410 --> 00:21:44.243
That's humbling.

00:21:47.740 --> 00:21:51.240
And then, for me, that's
kind of like the data,

00:21:51.240 --> 00:21:53.320
but again, what isn't taken into account

00:21:53.320 --> 00:21:55.187
at all in society, is like,
"Cool, you're a white dude

00:21:55.187 --> 00:21:57.541
"With a family," like
to me, having too much

00:21:57.541 --> 00:21:59.940
is the really, the other play.

00:21:59.940 --> 00:22:01.410
Like, what we don't have is a read on

00:22:01.410 --> 00:22:02.670
people's mental health, right?

00:22:02.670 --> 00:22:04.410
And so, I know I have that too,

00:22:04.410 --> 00:22:06.493
so now I'm just like, you know?

00:22:07.960 --> 00:22:09.220
I can't get into a funk,

00:22:09.220 --> 00:22:11.890
it's almost inappropriate, you know?

00:22:11.890 --> 00:22:15.930
Not that I force it, it's
just become me, you know.

00:22:15.930 --> 00:22:18.890
How many people work
out pretty religiously?

00:22:18.890 --> 00:22:21.780
Think about when you didn't,
think about when you started,

00:22:21.780 --> 00:22:23.090
and then, think about how it now feels

00:22:23.090 --> 00:22:24.743
completely foreign not to.

00:22:25.840 --> 00:22:27.140
That's what I do mentally.

00:22:30.730 --> 00:22:34.013
Like, just fucking
contextualize your problem,

00:22:35.610 --> 00:22:37.950
and let me give you a
really good piece of advice,

00:22:37.950 --> 00:22:40.950
pay very close attention to
who's listening to you complain.

00:22:43.160 --> 00:22:44.803
It's a very important tell.

00:22:46.740 --> 00:22:49.973
Like, I would shit on you
if you complained to me.

00:22:52.350 --> 00:22:54.210
So, pay close attention

00:22:54.210 --> 00:22:57.270
because when it's your
mom, or it's another friend

00:22:57.270 --> 00:22:59.870
that complains all the time,
you're in a bad system.

00:23:06.620 --> 00:23:09.650
- [Intern] In terms of, like,
judgments and criticism,

00:23:09.650 --> 00:23:12.200
where is the line of taking something

00:23:12.200 --> 00:23:15.680
as constructive criticism, and
then, just on the other side,

00:23:15.680 --> 00:23:19.147
being like, going with your
gut, and not listening to them?

00:23:19.147 --> 00:23:21.700
- I'd like you to be a
hell of a lot more closer

00:23:21.700 --> 00:23:24.820
to going with your gut
and not listening to them.

00:23:24.820 --> 00:23:26.840
Especially, at this age.

00:23:26.840 --> 00:23:29.550
At this age, you kind of
almost have to see it through

00:23:29.550 --> 00:23:31.603
to see how right or wrong you are.

00:23:33.870 --> 00:23:35.710
There's more upside in you figuring out

00:23:35.710 --> 00:23:36.960
if you're right or wrong.

00:23:38.330 --> 00:23:40.980
You can always take everybody's
feedback in your 30s.

00:23:44.050 --> 00:23:44.883
You know?

00:23:45.740 --> 00:23:48.120
Look, most people are just
gonna make assumptions,

00:23:48.120 --> 00:23:49.493
just based on your age.

00:23:50.620 --> 00:23:52.490
They're just giving you feedback
for the sake of feedback

00:23:52.490 --> 00:23:53.840
'cause you're 20-something.

00:23:57.150 --> 00:23:59.250
Now, if it's something like math,

00:23:59.250 --> 00:24:00.760
then, it's black and white, and
you get to the wrong answer,

00:24:00.760 --> 00:24:03.030
then, they were right
(laughs), like, you know?

00:24:03.030 --> 00:24:05.850
But like, but like, the people
here in analytics and media,

00:24:05.850 --> 00:24:07.960
like, that's like math,

00:24:07.960 --> 00:24:10.610
and like, you could fuckin'
have any ego you want,

00:24:10.610 --> 00:24:13.000
but if like it played out wrong, you lost,

00:24:13.000 --> 00:24:14.100
which is what's great about it.

00:24:14.100 --> 00:24:15.860
It's very, like, black and white,

00:24:15.860 --> 00:24:17.733
but all the other stuff is very gray,

00:24:19.130 --> 00:24:20.683
and becomes very subjective.

00:24:22.950 --> 00:24:26.683
The great foundation of my
life is being undereducated.

00:24:30.160 --> 00:24:33.790
It really worked for me,
just don't have that gear

00:24:33.790 --> 00:24:35.707
of seeing the world the same way.

00:24:42.048 --> 00:24:44.298
- Do you ever get stressed?

00:24:45.220 --> 00:24:48.550
- I almost wonder if, like,
what ended up happening was,

00:24:48.550 --> 00:24:50.060
you know, when you're a 22-year-old kid,

00:24:50.060 --> 00:24:51.990
and you walk into a family business,

00:24:51.990 --> 00:24:52.920
and you explode it,

00:24:52.920 --> 00:24:56.490
and you're wanted now from there on at 23,

00:24:56.490 --> 00:24:58.980
you're the foundation
of the financial success

00:24:58.980 --> 00:25:00.620
of your entire family,

00:25:00.620 --> 00:25:02.780
and you're the emotional
pillar of your family,

00:25:02.780 --> 00:25:04.040
along with your mother,

00:25:04.040 --> 00:25:06.400
so you're both financially
and emotionally responsible

00:25:06.400 --> 00:25:09.190
for everything you love at 23,

00:25:09.190 --> 00:25:10.800
you almost go into the other--

00:25:10.800 --> 00:25:14.220
I almost feel like I've been
in stress in perpetuity,

00:25:14.220 --> 00:25:15.653
that it's become happiness.

00:25:17.276 --> 00:25:20.160
(laughter)

00:25:20.160 --> 00:25:22.400
I don't know what else to say, you know?

00:25:22.400 --> 00:25:24.100
I don't feel stressed.

00:25:24.100 --> 00:25:26.550
I don't judge myself,
I feel like I'm trying.

00:25:26.550 --> 00:25:27.520
I know what my intent is,

00:25:27.520 --> 00:25:28.900
I feel like I'm trying really hard,

00:25:28.900 --> 00:25:30.917
and like, my big thing is like,

00:25:30.917 --> 00:25:33.480
before you say something to me,

00:25:33.480 --> 00:25:35.330
like, why don't you look at yourself?

00:25:36.240 --> 00:25:38.841
Like, it's really hard
for me to get going.

00:25:38.841 --> 00:25:43.210
Like, man, would I love for
all of you to be empowered

00:25:43.210 --> 00:25:45.220
with like, when your
parents say something,

00:25:45.220 --> 00:25:47.610
to call them out on them
doing the same thing.

00:25:47.610 --> 00:25:49.010
That's what parents do.

00:25:49.010 --> 00:25:51.270
They impose their thing on you.

00:25:51.270 --> 00:25:54.610
Your mom is doing what her mom did to her.

00:25:54.610 --> 00:25:57.490
Even though, she hated
that her mom did it to her.

00:25:57.490 --> 00:25:58.613
It's how humans work.

00:26:01.640 --> 00:26:04.083
Anyway, probably ...

00:26:06.130 --> 00:26:08.491
- But it just doesn't affect you?

00:26:08.491 --> 00:26:10.510
- I'm a big fan, of like,

00:26:10.510 --> 00:26:12.290
sleeping in the bed that you've made.

00:26:12.290 --> 00:26:15.270
I got big ambitions, I'm trying
to be out here doin' shit?

00:26:15.270 --> 00:26:16.103
Well, guess what?

00:26:16.103 --> 00:26:19.460
No one should be crying for
me, and so, I can't complain.

00:26:19.460 --> 00:26:22.640
I'm fucking, put myself out there, right?

00:26:22.640 --> 00:26:24.760
Like, I'm putting myself out there,

00:26:24.760 --> 00:26:26.150
I don't have to say yes to the interviews,

00:26:26.150 --> 00:26:28.130
I don't have to fuckin'
be filming my fuckin' self

00:26:28.130 --> 00:26:29.410
24 hours a day.

00:26:29.410 --> 00:26:31.460
I made this bed, I gotta sleep in it,

00:26:31.460 --> 00:26:32.720
everything that comes along with it,

00:26:32.720 --> 00:26:35.440
and if I do something
fucking stupid or wrong,

00:26:35.440 --> 00:26:38.040
and I get called out on it,
I need to own that shit,

00:26:38.040 --> 00:26:40.960
and I'm okay with that 'cause
I don't think I'm perfect.

00:26:40.960 --> 00:26:42.510
I'm just trying to stay narrow.

00:26:43.370 --> 00:26:45.460
Narrow is my thing, you know?

00:26:45.460 --> 00:26:47.510
Stay in my lane, you know?

00:26:47.510 --> 00:26:48.557
That's why I don't talk, you
know, a lot of people are like,

00:26:48.557 --> 00:26:49.560
"You don't talk about politics."

00:26:49.560 --> 00:26:51.217
I'm like, "I wanna do homework on it,

00:26:51.217 --> 00:26:53.000
"I don't wanna just shoot my opinion."

00:26:53.000 --> 00:26:54.910
It's like, like, you know?

00:26:54.910 --> 00:26:56.550
I've got opinions,

00:26:56.550 --> 00:26:58.790
but like now, like, I just
think about all those things,

00:26:58.790 --> 00:27:00.500
or about anything, anything.

00:27:00.500 --> 00:27:01.970
I need to be thoughtful.

00:27:01.970 --> 00:27:05.370
I stay very narrow, chess.

00:27:05.370 --> 00:27:06.633
Two moves ...

00:27:07.700 --> 00:27:08.860
I do so many things

00:27:08.860 --> 00:27:11.113
that are actually setting
up something else,

00:27:12.640 --> 00:27:17.460
but I get scrutinized, judged,
or viewed on the first move.

00:27:17.460 --> 00:27:20.203
A jab isn't trying to knock
somebody out in a fight.

00:27:22.660 --> 00:27:24.290
I'm really good at jabbing.

00:27:24.290 --> 00:27:26.740
I'm really thoughtful,
I'm playing the long game.

00:27:30.607 --> 00:27:32.140
- [Intern] So, I know you've
got an experience team,

00:27:32.140 --> 00:27:34.470
and I've had some time

00:27:34.470 --> 00:27:37.130
to look into like exit interview trends,

00:27:37.130 --> 00:27:38.950
and I'd love to hear
from your perspective,

00:27:38.950 --> 00:27:41.410
like, when you read
what someone had to say

00:27:41.410 --> 00:27:44.160
about what it was like to
work here and work for you,

00:27:44.160 --> 00:27:45.609
what's the best thing and the worst thing

00:27:45.609 --> 00:27:46.859
that someone's ever said?

00:27:49.220 --> 00:27:51.050
- I mean, in the most extremes,

00:27:51.050 --> 00:27:53.210
it's like I'm devastated I'm leaving.

00:27:53.210 --> 00:27:55.357
It's because of some
outrageous circumstance,

00:27:55.357 --> 00:27:56.487
"like my parents are sick,

00:27:56.487 --> 00:27:59.367
"or my significant other's
getting transferred,

00:27:59.367 --> 00:28:02.137
"and like, this is
breaking my heart and soul.

00:28:02.137 --> 00:28:04.140
"I wanna work here forever."

00:28:04.140 --> 00:28:05.395
And in the other direction is,

00:28:05.395 --> 00:28:07.050
this whole thing is full of shit,

00:28:07.050 --> 00:28:08.763
you're full of shit, fuck you.

00:28:09.610 --> 00:28:10.743
I've been undermined,

00:28:12.150 --> 00:28:12.990
you're completely full of shit,

00:28:12.990 --> 00:28:15.700
fuck you, fuck you, you're full of shit.

00:28:15.700 --> 00:28:17.150
Those are the best and worst.

00:28:18.610 --> 00:28:19.700
Yeah, it's super tough,

00:28:19.700 --> 00:28:22.060
but what ends up
happening, back to stress,

00:28:22.060 --> 00:28:24.290
or this and that, it's, I have empathy.

00:28:24.290 --> 00:28:26.393
By the way, they might be right.

00:28:27.810 --> 00:28:29.220
Like, I'm not crippled by the fact

00:28:29.220 --> 00:28:31.800
that three people right
now could be terrible,

00:28:31.800 --> 00:28:33.260
and like, serendipitously,

00:28:33.260 --> 00:28:35.630
got teamed up, created
a triangle of terror,

00:28:35.630 --> 00:28:38.640
and I haven't been able
to, like, weed it out yet.

00:28:38.640 --> 00:28:41.450
The best part is though,
I don't want that.

00:28:41.450 --> 00:28:42.490
Look, you were in the meeting

00:28:42.490 --> 00:28:43.960
where I literally told the entire team,

00:28:43.960 --> 00:28:45.293
like, from now on,

00:28:46.200 --> 00:28:48.170
any time somebody's crying somewhere,

00:28:48.170 --> 00:28:50.060
I need to know on the moment it happens.

00:28:50.060 --> 00:28:51.640
You saw it, not for show for the inter--

00:28:51.640 --> 00:28:52.650
You were in the meeting.

00:28:52.650 --> 00:28:54.840
- [Intern] Yeah, I mean--
- That's who I am.

00:28:54.840 --> 00:28:56.730
Like, so like, I sleep well at night.

00:28:56.730 --> 00:28:59.180
Triangle of terror, and somebody
says I'm a piece of shit,

00:28:59.180 --> 00:29:00.630
and this is all a facade.

00:29:00.630 --> 00:29:01.463
I can live with that

00:29:01.463 --> 00:29:03.410
because I understand that could be true.

00:29:03.410 --> 00:29:05.260
Four offices, 900 people,

00:29:05.260 --> 00:29:06.760
even though, I'm on the
pulse of every minute.

00:29:06.760 --> 00:29:08.400
I'm trying out here, but like,

00:29:08.400 --> 00:29:11.523
I'm not fuckin', you
know, like, omnipresent.

00:29:13.675 --> 00:29:15.920
(laughter)
You know, and so, so ...

00:29:15.920 --> 00:29:19.070
I just feel great with
my intent and my efforts.

00:29:19.070 --> 00:29:22.820
I know I'm not making
financial decisions like 99.9.

00:29:22.820 --> 00:29:24.900
Of course I am, 'cause
we have to make payroll,

00:29:24.900 --> 00:29:27.100
but I'm not trying to
like, maximize profits,

00:29:27.100 --> 00:29:30.400
so I can buy a helicopter
and feel good about myself,

00:29:30.400 --> 00:29:33.360
and so, like, I just know I'm
way ahead of everything else,

00:29:33.360 --> 00:29:35.550
and I read all that stuff,
I read everyone of those.

00:29:35.550 --> 00:29:36.450
You know, I get them right away.

00:29:36.450 --> 00:29:38.760
I fuckin' read, talk
about, I don't read shit.

00:29:38.760 --> 00:29:41.080
I read every word, I try to synthesize it.

00:29:41.080 --> 00:29:42.480
I email every one of them,

00:29:42.480 --> 00:29:44.900
which is why we have
their non-Vayner email.

00:29:44.900 --> 00:29:47.130
I try to, like, let them know

00:29:47.130 --> 00:29:49.110
that I'm here for them in perpetuity.

00:29:49.110 --> 00:29:51.530
If anybody ever works
at Vayner, for you guys,

00:29:51.530 --> 00:29:53.530
just being an intern, if I
can help you in 19 years.

00:29:53.530 --> 00:29:56.585
You email me, be like, "I was
an intern in fucking 2018,

00:29:56.585 --> 00:29:58.647
"and da-da-da," and like, you know,

00:29:58.647 --> 00:30:00.806
"now, my aunt wants to be--"

00:30:00.806 --> 00:30:02.240
You know, I don't give a fuck.

00:30:02.240 --> 00:30:06.700
Just feel free to reach out,
and so, intent trumps a lot.

00:30:06.700 --> 00:30:08.690
You have to execute on your intent.

00:30:08.690 --> 00:30:10.440
You know, a lot of my closest friends

00:30:10.440 --> 00:30:12.277
kinda scrutinize, they're
like, "Intent, intent."

00:30:12.277 --> 00:30:13.447
"you could intend to be a good guy,

00:30:13.447 --> 00:30:15.160
"but shoot somebody with a gun, and then,"

00:30:15.160 --> 00:30:16.250
I'm like, I get it,

00:30:16.250 --> 00:30:20.413
but usually intent maps to
your actions, and so ...

00:30:24.350 --> 00:30:26.311
- [Intern] Where do you
draw inspiration from?

00:30:26.311 --> 00:30:27.144
When you started Vayner

00:30:27.144 --> 00:30:29.293
and then also, how you make
your decisions correctly?

00:30:30.690 --> 00:30:31.673
- I want legacy.

00:30:32.530 --> 00:30:35.550
It's very insular, it's
not from somebody else.

00:30:35.550 --> 00:30:38.010
You know, my inspiration is ...

00:30:38.010 --> 00:30:41.970
I'm aware that something
really good happened

00:30:41.970 --> 00:30:45.070
when I was produced, and
I wanna see it through.

00:30:45.070 --> 00:30:47.983
I think I owe it to my
parents, I think I owe it to,

00:30:49.040 --> 00:30:51.120
I'm just very driven by gratitude.

00:30:51.120 --> 00:30:52.860
I'm very inspired by it.

00:30:52.860 --> 00:30:57.590
I know good things happen,
mentally, really this, you know?

00:30:57.590 --> 00:30:59.310
And I need to see it through.

00:30:59.310 --> 00:31:02.260
You know, there's a curiosity
of how big can this be

00:31:02.260 --> 00:31:04.823
that really fucks with me, in a good way.

00:31:06.200 --> 00:31:09.753
Like, I really, really, really
think I'm gonna be Oprah.

00:31:11.071 --> 00:31:13.023
(laughter)
Like for real,

00:31:14.170 --> 00:31:16.670
which is amazing, like,
a singular individual

00:31:16.670 --> 00:31:18.510
for a moment in time.

00:31:18.510 --> 00:31:20.130
You know, it's really
interesting too, to me,

00:31:20.130 --> 00:31:22.337
and I think about psycho, like
I analyze things, I'm like,

00:31:22.337 --> 00:31:24.437
"If I'm right, and this
happens in nine years,

00:31:24.437 --> 00:31:25.720
"it's gonna be an interesting time."

00:31:25.720 --> 00:31:28.610
Like, in a world where, like, white dudes,

00:31:28.610 --> 00:31:30.180
are like demonized, and
things of that nature,

00:31:30.180 --> 00:31:31.914
like, is it important that--

00:31:31.914 --> 00:31:34.000
Like, I think about the
fact that I'm penetrating

00:31:34.000 --> 00:31:37.660
15-25 dudes and I'm pushing
empathy and kindness,

00:31:37.660 --> 00:31:38.800
like, is this all meant to be?

00:31:38.800 --> 00:31:41.070
You know, I get into
that, I definitely do.

00:31:41.070 --> 00:31:43.640
I'm like, I'm such an alpha male,

00:31:43.640 --> 00:31:47.653
yet, like, what I preach
is so traditionally soft,

00:31:48.720 --> 00:31:50.540
and like, I get to live my life.

00:31:50.540 --> 00:31:52.590
Like, I get to be like, in an airport,

00:31:52.590 --> 00:31:54.247
and see a hood dude roll
up to me and be like,

00:31:54.247 --> 00:31:56.365
"Yo, gratitude, dawg."

00:31:56.365 --> 00:31:58.360
(laughter)
You know, it's like, it's fun.

00:31:58.360 --> 00:31:59.580
It's like, I'm seeing the piece,

00:31:59.580 --> 00:32:01.600
I'm seeing the puzzle come together

00:32:01.600 --> 00:32:04.520
while being unbelievable comfortable

00:32:04.520 --> 00:32:07.200
in my capitalistic, you know, ambition.

00:32:07.200 --> 00:32:09.150
You know, I think I'm a very funny read,

00:32:09.150 --> 00:32:10.770
even as I read myself.

00:32:10.770 --> 00:32:13.430
It's like, I've got all
this underlying stuff,

00:32:13.430 --> 00:32:15.147
but the thing that most
people know is like,

00:32:15.147 --> 00:32:16.270
"He wants to buy the jets,"

00:32:16.270 --> 00:32:18.424
which is very like douchey, and like,

00:32:18.424 --> 00:32:20.190
the whole thing's super interesting to me,

00:32:20.190 --> 00:32:22.507
and I'm always like, "Am
I sandbagging everybody?

00:32:22.507 --> 00:32:23.577
"Like, is that what I put out?

00:32:23.577 --> 00:32:25.067
"Like, is that why I speak that way?

00:32:25.067 --> 00:32:27.217
"Like, why is it that they like me more

00:32:27.217 --> 00:32:28.530
"as they get closer, not further?"

00:32:28.530 --> 00:32:30.500
Like, it's all very interesting to me.

00:32:30.500 --> 00:32:33.870
I see my patterns, it's
the underdog thing.

00:32:33.870 --> 00:32:34.910
You know, like, why will I turn on,

00:32:34.910 --> 00:32:36.550
like a golf match, give a fuck,

00:32:36.550 --> 00:32:38.580
but like whoever's the
underdog, that's who I'm for.

00:32:38.580 --> 00:32:40.210
Like, fuckin' MAC Conference

00:32:40.210 --> 00:32:44.080
college basketball game at
night, turn on underdog, right?

00:32:44.080 --> 00:32:45.420
Like, Kobe, hate him.

00:32:45.420 --> 00:32:47.110
Last season, washed up, love him.

00:32:47.110 --> 00:32:50.180
Like, I'm very like, am
I doing that to myself?

00:32:50.180 --> 00:32:52.390
Like, do I like the fact that
